      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the voiced alveolar fricative /z/, your vocal cords vibrate rapidly while air is forced through a narrow constriction at the teeth and alveolus, creating a buzzing sound. In contrast, the unvoiced counterpart /s/ is produced with the same airflow and tongue position, but your vocal cords remain relaxed and do not vibrate, resulting in a sharper, hissing noise. The primary physical distinction lies solely in the vocal folding vibration: /z/ uses phonation (vibration), while /s/ uses phonationless airflow (no vibration).",
